vi with UTF-8 text. busybox vi knows only bytes: anything from 0x80 up is drawn as '.' (or, with
FEATURE_VI_8BIT, passed through but counted as one column a byte), the cursor moves a byte at a
time, and x, r or a backspace remove one byte of a multibyte character, which corrupts the file.
Korean text showed as rows of dots and edited into garbage.

With Unicode support on (UNICODE_SUPPORT, and unicode_status ON), text[] is taken as UTF-8:

- a character is a valid UTF-8 sequence plus the combining (zero width) characters after it;
  next_char/prev_char/char_start step over one. Bytes that are not valid UTF-8 stay one
  character each and are drawn as '.', as before.
- the screen buffer is one cell per column (cell_t: the bytes drawn there; an empty cell is the
  right half of a wide character), so a wide (CJK) character takes 2 columns, refresh() redraws
  it whole, and a wide character cut by the left or right edge of a scrolled line shows as
  '<' or '>'. Columns (cursor, j/k keeping the column, |, autoindent) count real widths; the
  cursor sits on a wide character's first column.
- h/l, x/X/s, the Delete key, r, R, a, backspace in insert mode, Esc, and the end of an operator's
  range (de, d$, dl, ...) take whole characters. In Replace mode the first byte of a typed
  character replaces the character under the cursor and the rest are inserted after it; a
  backspace puts the replaced character back. The undo queue keeps a multibyte backspace in one
  object (it used to drop deletions longer than a byte).
- word motions (w, b, e, E) count non-ASCII characters as word characters, and step a whole
  character, so they do not stop inside one (e used to stick at the end of a Korean word).
- f/F/t/T and r read a whole UTF-8 character; the backspace on the : / ? line removes one;
  :list and "not implemented" messages show printable UTF-8 as it is.

Without Unicode support the code paths reduce to the byte-wise ones (utf8_char() says every byte
is a character of width 1).

